// POSITIVE control, not a negative — and a correction recorded the same way
// `unikraft/alloc`'s README records its own. My first draft of this file was
// named `negative_object_never_discharged.kz` and claimed dropping a live
// <held!> binding without an explicit `release` call must not compile. That
// claim was WRONG, disproved by compiling this exact program: `<held!>` has
// exactly ONE unattended terminal disposer (`release`), so auto-discharge
// silently INSERTS the call rather than reporting a leak — the same
// mechanism `unikraft/alloc` documents for its own `<live!>` state, keyed by
// binding name, not by arm position (`src/auto_discharge_inserter.zig`).
//
// Verified by reading the emitted Zig: this source contains ZERO literal
// `unikraft/store:release` calls, and `output_emitted.zig` contains exactly
// ONE `release_event.handler(.{ .obj = held })` call. Compile, then `grep
// release_event.handler output_emitted.zig` to reproduce.
//
// So, precisely: "forgetting to release a held object is a compile error" is
// NOT a claim this lift can make — it is a compile-time INSERTION. The
// negatives that DO hold are `negative_release_without_add.kz` (a genuine
// state-mismatch refusal: `release` structurally cannot accept <built!>) and
// `negative_release_twice.kz` (use-after-discharge on an ALREADY-inserted or
// explicit release).

~std/compiler:paths {
    unikraft: {{ ENTRY }}/../..
}

~import unikraft/store

~unikraft/store:alloc(name: "auto", id: 4)
| ready obj |> unikraft/store:add(obj)
    | ok held |> _
    | refused e |> unikraft/store:abandon(e.obj)
| err _ |> _
